<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Hardened Runtime entitlements for the signed Digi Setu .app.

    Hardened Runtime (codesign with the runtime option) is mandatory
    for Apple notarization. By default it blocks JIT, dlopen of
    unsigned libraries, and DYLD_* env vars; all three of which our
    bundle legitimately needs:

      * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      * com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
            The bundled Node.js (V8) needs writable executable memory
            to JIT-compile JavaScript. Without these two, every
            invocation of the `pi` rpc subprocess crashes with
            EXC_BAD_ACCESS the moment V8 tries to allocate its
            JIT page.

      * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
            The PyInstaller-frozen sidecar dlopen()s ~50 .dylib /
            .so files at runtime (pydantic_core, _cffi_backend,
            cryptography, lxml, …). They were signed by their
            upstream maintainers (or not at all), NOT by us, so
            Hardened Runtime's library-validation rejects them and
            the sidecar dies in the import phase with
            "code signature invalid". Disabling library validation
            opts out of that check for this binary specifically.

      * com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
            PyInstaller's bootloader sets DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH (and on
            some hosts DYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH) to the unpacked
            _MEIPASS dir so the frozen .dylibs resolve. Hardened
            Runtime strips DYLD_* by default — this entitlement
            opts back in.

    Notably ABSENT (deliberately):
      * com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection
            We don't need to disable W^X enforcement; V8 uses the
            allowed JIT pathway via the two `allow-*` entitlements
            above. Disabling page protection would weaken our own
            security for no benefit.
      * com.apple.security.cs.allow-relative-library-loads
      * com.apple.security.cs.allow-network-client/server
            Network access doesn't require an entitlement on macOS
            for non-sandboxed apps. Only sandboxed (Mac App Store)
            apps need explicit network entitlements.
      * com.apple.security.app-sandbox
            We ship outside the Mac App Store via Developer ID,
            so the app sandbox is intentionally disabled — a
            sandboxed app can't pick the user's folder via
            tauri-plugin-dialog and run subprocesses against it,
            which is the entire point of the desktop client.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
